            <ArticleTitle>Psychiatric disorders and risk of transition to chronicity in men with first onset low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1391-400</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4637.2010.00934.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess whether pre-existing psychiatric diagnoses increase the likelihood of transitioning from sub-acute to chronic back pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective cohort study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Men (N = 140) experiencing a first onset of low back pain (LBP) were examined for lifetime psychiatric disorders approximately 8 weeks post pain-onset using the Diagnostic Interview Schedule (DIS-III-R), then re-evaluated at 6 months after pain onset to determine who did or did not progress to pain chronicity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Transition to chronic pain and disability was based on 6-month self-report measures of pain intensity and perceived disability.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Men with a pre-pain lifetime diagnosis of major depressive disorder had 5 times greater risk of transitioning to chronic LBP (odds ratio [OR] = 4.99; 95% confidence interval [CI] 1.49-16.76). Increased risk was also associated with a pre-pain lifetime diagnosis of generalized anxiety (OR = 2.45; 95% CI 1.06-5.68), post-traumatic stress (OR = 3.23; 95% CI 1.11-9.44), and with current nicotine dependence (OR = 2.49; 95% CI 1.15-5.40). There were no statistically significant effects for abuse or dependence of alcohol or other psychoactive substances.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Lifetime history of major depression or a major anxiety disorder may represent potential psychosocial &quot;yellow flags&quot; for the transition to chronicity in men with first-onset LBP. Screening for lifetime depressive or anxiety disorders may identify individuals at higher risk, who may benefit from referral for more intensive rehabilitation.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
